course: dictionary
lesson: 12213
title: Guesses, not proof
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how sure a writer is. Conjectural is one of them. It means based on a guess, not on firm facts or proof. «The date of the old map is conjectural.» The writer is telling you that nobody knows the date for certain, and the date is a good guess.
explain: Check it with the swap test. Put in a simpler word, like guessed, and read the sentence again: «The date of the old map is guessed.» It still makes sense, so guessed is a good meaning for conjectural. Look at the words around it for more clues about how sure the writer is.
explain: Some words add strength to conjectural. «The story about the lost city is purely conjectural» means there is nothing but a guess. «The plan was highly conjectural» means it is based on a lot of guessing. Purely means only, and highly means very much.
explain: In maths, a conjecture is an idea that people think is true but nobody has proved yet. Mathematicians can spend many years trying to prove one. Until they do, it stays conjectural, however sensible it sounds.
fact: Conjectural means based on a guess or an idea that has not been proved.
fact: «Conjectural» is often used in history and science to show that something is a guess, such as what dinosaurs looked like.
fact: A conjecture is a statement that people think is true but nobody has proved yet.
fact: Phrases like «purely conjectural» and «highly conjectural» show how much guessing is involved.
fact: The swap test helps: put in guessed or an idea nobody has proved, and check that the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The colour of the dinosaurs is conjectural, because no one has found their skin.» What does conjectural mean here? | *Based on a guess, not proved | Painted in bright colours | Found in a museum | Sure and certain
exercise: Scientists say the date of the map is conjectural. What does this tell you about the date? | It has been firmly proved by three experts | *It is a guess and not proved | It is written in gold ink | It is always right
exercise: Which of these is a conjectural idea? | *Aliens may have visited the ancient city, but no one has found any proof | The sun rises in the east | Water boils when it gets very hot | Two plus two is four
test: In «a highly conjectural guess», how strong is the guessing? | Nobody knows | Not at all | Only a little | *Very much
test: Ben says, «The name of the lost city is purely conjectural.» What does purely add to the meaning? | A pure white colour | Proof from three scientists | *Only a guess, with nothing proved | A friendly, kind tone
exam: A maths teacher says a conjecture is an idea thought to be true but never proved. Which choice matches that idea? | A proof that everyone agrees on | A rule that is always wrong | *A statement thought to be true but not yet proved | A type of number
